          "id": "the_four_worked_examples_reach_MOVESOutput",
          "description": "docs/expand-day.md §6.1-§6.4, asserted against the numbers MOVESOutput stores rather than against this document's own. FOUR COHORTS x TWO DAY TYPES, which is the shape the day correction took away from every other fixture in this tree: A (MY 1980, gasoline) is the largest rate and shows every factor at once, B (MY 2020, gasoline) is the largest activity, C (MY 2000, electricity) exercises the EV efficiency divisor at its largest -- 22.1% -- and D (MY 2020, electricity) at the other end of the age-group ladder.\n\nEACH PAIR IS THE SAME COHORT ON THE TWO DAYS, so the pair pins a RATIO that no single-day fixture can state. For A it is 1.46683 / 0.452942 = 3.2384, and it factorises exactly: (333651 / 392141) x (23.1918 / 2.43728) x (2 / 5) -- base rate, source hours and the RECIPROCAL of noOfRealDays. A port that hardcoded noOfRealDays = 5 leaves the day-5 half of this test green and multiplies the day-2 half by 2.5; a port that dropped the divisor entirely leaves neither.\n\n`output_rows` is (cohort, day) with the day INNERMOST -- measured, per docs/esm-conventions.md §42.3, by emitting out_dayID down the ordinals rather than derived -- so odd ordinals are dayID 2 and even ones dayID 5, and each pair here is 2c-1 and 2c. (It was day-outermost when these were first written; each worked example was re-pinned to the row its model year and fuel type now occupy.) The tolerance is the fixture's own per-cell gate, because MOVESOutput stores six significant figures and §7.1 attributes every residual here to that. The three SCCs are exact integers and are asserted exactly: an SCC off by one digit relabels a whole fuel type and still passes a per-pollutant sum.",
